---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<Base
  title="Mac"
  description="Furlough for Mac is a separate download, signed and notarized by Apple. The same budgets, windows and Anchor, enforced the Mac's own way."
>
  <section class="wrap page">
    <header class="head" data-reveal>
      <div class="eyebrow amber">Furlough · for Mac</div>
      <h1 class="display">Furlough for Mac.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A separate app, downloaded from here rather than the App Store. The same budgets, windows
        and Anchor, enforced the Mac's own way — Apple's Screen Time API doesn't exist on macOS.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="glass large prominent get" href={site.macDownloadURL} rel="noopener">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="currentColor">
            <path fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd" d="M6 4a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v8.5h16V6a2 2 0 0 0-2-2H6zm-.5 2a.5.5 0 0 1 .5-.5h12a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v7H5.5V6z"/>
            <path d="M2 16.5h20l-.9 2.1a2 2 0 0 1-1.84 1.22H4.74a2 2 0 0 1-1.84-1.22L2 16.5z"/>
          </svg>
          Download Furlough {site.macVersion}
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="fine faint">
        A {site.macVersion} disk image, signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple.
        Requires macOS {site.macMinimumOS} or later. Open it and drag Furlough to Applications.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="card prose body" data-reveal style="--d: .1s">
      <div class="note">
        <p><strong>The App Store listing is the iPhone app.</strong> It installs on an Apple
        silicon Mac and blocks nothing there. Apple's Screen Time API — the thing that shields an
        app on an iPhone — is unavailable on macOS, so the iPhone build launches on a Mac and has
        nothing to enforce with. This download is the Mac app, and it is the one that works.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Why it isn't on the Mac App Store</h2>
      <p>To enforce anything on a Mac, Furlough has to do the work Screen Time does on a phone: ask
      a blocked app to quit, read which site a browser tab is showing, and run a network filter.
      The Mac App Store requires the App Sandbox without exception, and a sandboxed app can do
      none of those things. So the Mac build is distributed the other way Apple supports — a
      Developer ID signature and notarization — from this page.</p>

      <h2>What macOS will ask you for</h2>
      <p>Because it does its own enforcing, Furlough needs permissions the iPhone app never has to
      ask for. macOS will prompt for these, and the app cannot block anything without them:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A system extension.</strong> The website blocking runs as a network extension,
        and macOS asks you to allow it in System Settings the first time.</li>
        <li><strong>Permission to filter network content.</strong> The extension is a content
        filter, so macOS asks separately before it can start.</li>
        <li><strong>Automation, once per browser.</strong> Furlough reads the current tab of each
        running browser to see whether it is showing a blocked site, and redirects it if it is.
        macOS asks the first time it talks to Safari, then again for each other browser.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Worth knowing before you install</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Furlough refuses to quit while anything is shielded — that is the Mac's version of
        having no unblock button. Logging out, restarting and shutting down are let through.</li>
        <li>Force Quit is the documented way out, the same way the Screen Time switch is on the
        iPhone. It is deliberately not a comfortable one: a background agent reopens Furlough
        shortly afterwards.</li>
        <li>The Mac app works on its own. It does not need the iPhone app, and the iPhone app does
        not need it — though the Anchor can be paired across both.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Or build it yourself</h2>
      <p>Furlough for Mac is open source. If you would rather compile it than trust a download,
      <a href={site.githubURL} rel="noopener">the source is on GitHub</a>.</p>

      <p class="contact">
        {site.appStoreURL && <a href={site.appStoreURL} rel="noopener">Furlough for iPhone, on the App Store</a>}
        {site.appStoreURL && ' · '}
        <a href="/support/">Support</a> · <a href="/privacy/">Privacy policy</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page { padding: clamp(24px, 6vh, 64px) 0 0; max-width: 760px; }
  .head h1 { margin-top: 12px; font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 56px); }
  .head .lede { margin-top: 16px; }
  .actions { margin-top: 26px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  /* Carries `prominent` outright rather than `data-plat`: anyone who reached this page asked for
     the Mac app, so the download leads here even when the visitor is on an iPhone. */
  .get { min-height: 56px; padding: 0 24px; font-size: 16px; }
  .get svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; }
  .head .fine { margin-top: 14px; font-size: 13px; }
  .body { margin-top: 28px; padding: clamp(22px, 4vw, 36px); }
  .contact { margin-top: 32px; padding-top: 18px; border-top: 1px solid var(--edge); font-size: 14px; }
</style>
